{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said to Abraham, etc.</b> One needs to know why it says the word <b>zeh</b>, and further why it says the word <b>leimor</b>. Also the entire verse is superfluous, for it should have said nothing other than ״לָמָּה צָחֲקָה״." "<b>Indeed,</b> the intent is that we find in the preceding parashah that Abraham too laughed when HaShem told him the glad tidings of the seed, as it is written (Genesis 17:17) ״וַיִּפֹּל אַבְרָהָם… וַיִּצְחָק״, and we did not see that HaShem took offense that Abraham laughed. And one might say: ״How did Sarah's laughter differ from Abraham's laughter?״ And we find that Onkelos intended to resolve this by translating Abraham's laughter as ״וְחַדִי״ (rejoiced) and Sarah's laughter as ״וְחַיֵּכַת״ (mocked), but on what basis did the Holy One, blessed be He, perceive two different meanings in one word?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the correct explanation is that the Holy One, blessed be He, was precise in His words regarding the reason for His displeasure with Sarah but not with Abraham. And you will find that Abraham laughed at the time of the glad tidings, whereas Sarah did not laugh at the time she was informed, but rather at the time she saw her renewed youth, as it says ״וְתִצְחַק שָׂרָה… אַחֲרֵי בְלוֹתִי הָיְתָה לִּי עֶדְנָה״ — this tells us that the promise of childbirth was not confirmed for her until she saw the physical change, and it was over this that HaShem took offense. And that is His saying <b>לָמָּה זֶּה צָחֲקָה</b> — meaning: over this laughter I take offense, and not over your laughter. And the reason is that this laughter of hers was intended to say ״הַאַף אֻמְנָם״ — it became confirmed for her only after the fact of childbirth, which shows that she did not believe and did not rely upon My promise to her, because it is a wondrous thing, ״הֲיִפָּלֵא מֵי־הֳוָה דָּבָר״ etc." "<b>And I am grown old, etc.</b> It is difficult — how could HaShem, blessed be He, utter from His mouth something that was not so? For even though the Sages, of blessed memory, said (Bava Metzia 87a) that from here we learn that one may alter [the truth] for the sake of peace, nevertheless relative to the exalted truthfulness of the Blessed One, from whom true utterances proceed, He would not, heaven forbid, say what was not so. And according to what we explained — that the displeasure was because she did not believe she would give birth on account of her old age, as she said ״אַחֲרֵי בְלוֹתִי״, until she saw that she had returned to the days of her youth — HaShem is true and His words are true. And one may precisely say that the reason He took offense at her was that she did not believe before she was renewed, for even though Sarah added further ״וַאדֹנִי זָקֵן״, this was after she had been justified by the promise, for she had already experienced renewed youth: צָחֲקָה means she rejoiced, and she sang song and praise to El Elyon that after her old age she had been granted renewed youth — which showed that she had returned to the days of her youth to give birth. And she added to magnify the kindness of El by saying ״וַאדֹנִי זָקֵן״, and nevertheless the wonders of El were performed upon her, for it is obvious that after she saw her renewed youth she would no longer question the matter, and she said ״וַאדֹנִי זָקֵן״ only to give thanks for His wonders."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:10.289716143Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}